\begin{abstract}
% SPEC s3 with revision T7 (A34): result and replay scope first (absolute before relative), then lever, design, H1 to H3, retained share, history prediction, weak/null and bounds.
We replayed three real high-performance computing (HPC) job traces (PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2) counterfactually on 42 U.S. balancing authorities over a held-out year. Delaying jobs by up to 12 h saved 6.6, 13.2 and 5.3 g CO$_2$e per kWh of job energy on the three traces, respectively. The planner used a past-only LightGBM forecast and the requested walltime; these post-hoc savings convert pre-registered relative savings of 1.9\,\%, 3.8\,\% and 1.5\,\% of run-now emissions. Delaying batch jobs to low-carbon hours can cut emissions, but a scheduler in use sees only a past-only forecast and the requested walltime; this pre-registered measurement proposes no new scheduler. Tests of the saving, the cost of the requested walltime and the recovery by a history-based runtime prediction (H1 to H3) are supported on PM100 ($p<0.0005$, Holm-adjusted $<0.0015$); the other traces are secondary replications. The same planner given perfect information saved more, and the forecast-and-request policy retained 0.33, 0.53 and 0.38 of that saving. A runtime prediction from the user's recent jobs raised the saving on all three traces. The Shapley split of the remaining gap is descriptive and trace-specific, and night-only and persistence rules saved little or increased emissions. The savings are bounds under free capacity, use production-average intensity rather than avoided marginal emissions, and carry intervals conditional on the sampled workload. The replay suggests that runtime information may be worth evaluating in production schedulers.
\end{abstract}

\begin{highlights}
\item A past-only forecast with requested walltimes saves carbon in three HPC replays
\item The forecast-and-request policy keeps part of the perfect-information saving
\item A history-based runtime prediction raises the replay saving on all three traces
\item Pre-registered replay on 42 U.S. grids, a held-out year and three real HPC traces
\item Night-only and persistence rules saved little or increased emissions
\end{highlights}

