===== 01_intro =====
% 01_intro.tmpl.tex: SPEC s5 "1 Introduction" (950 words; five paragraphs plus contributions).
% Points: (1) stake and lever; (2) what is known and what a deployed scheduler sees; (3) gap (LESSONS L1 shape);
% (4) finding and framing (about 150 words); (5) definitions; (6) contributions (nominal voice, no numbers).
% Figures: Fig 1 (fig:design), first cited at the end of point 4. Tables: none. Equations: none. Algorithms: none.
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Computing draws a notable share of electricity. Data-center energy use grew far more slowly than workloads up to 2018 \cite{masanet2020}, but high-performance computing (HPC) systems, which serve queues of batch jobs on thousands of nodes, add a carbon footprint that recent estimates describe as significant \cite{li2023sc}. Operational emissions per kilowatt-hour are not constant, because the carbon intensity of grid electricity changes by the hour with the generation mix, and the same job therefore emits different amounts of CO$_2$ depending on when and where it runs \cite{dodge2022}. A batch scheduler can use this variation without new hardware, because many jobs tolerate a late start and the scheduler may hold a job, within a window of slack, until a lower-carbon hour. Doing so requires two inputs that a deployed scheduler must supply for itself: a forecast of carbon intensity and an estimate of how long each job will run.

Temporal shifting of this kind is well studied. Simulation studies place delay-tolerant jobs at low-carbon hours \cite{wiesner2021,sukprasert2024limits,suscom_101498,suscom_101407,cucumber2022}, a production system shifts flexible load with day-ahead forecasts \cite{radovanovic2023}, and the forecast itself has been developed as a separate problem \cite{maji2022carboncast,joseph2025}. These studies mostly give the scheduler the true carbon signal or a noise-perturbed copy of it, and most give it the true job length. Wiesner et al.\ compare an optimal signal with forecasts that carry injected error, Sukprasert et al.\ include a prediction-error scenario, and Attenni et al.\ state that runtimes are assumed available to the scheduler \cite{wiesner2021,sukprasert2024limits,suscom_101498}. The distance between a forecast-driven and a perfect-information scheduler is therefore already known; ReBase reports it by forecast horizon for wind-powered data centers \cite{suscom_101470}.

A deployed HPC scheduler faces a different input set. Its forecast is built from past data only, and its runtime information is the walltime that the user requested at submission, an upper bound that the system enforces by killing the job. On the three traces used here, the median analyzed job ran for 14.3\,\%, 30.6\,\% and 17.8\,\% of its requested time (PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2). Older logs show the same looseness: the mean over jobs of runtime divided by the estimate, called accuracy, was between 31 and 47 percent on four production logs \cite{tsafrir2007}.

Studies that vary forecast error inject synthetic noise or assume ideal job lengths, and studies on real job traces assume known runtimes \cite{wiesner2021,sukprasert2024limits,suscom_101498,bostandoost2024,feng2026,joseph2025}. The saving that a scheduler realizes with a trained past-only forecast and the user-requested walltime, and how the lost saving divides between those two inputs, is not reported in the studies we reviewed. A saving that holds only under perfect inputs is an upper bound, and a decision to assess carbon-aware delay needs the saving that survives imperfect inputs.

That saving is measured in a pre-registered replay of three real HPC job traces on 42 U.S. balancing authorities over a held-out year. At 12 h of slack, delay scheduling with a past-only LightGBM forecast and the requested walltime saved 6.6, 13.2 and 5.3 g CO$_2$e per kWh of job energy on PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2 (a post-hoc conversion of the pre-registered relative saving). This forecast-and-request policy is called the deployable arm $D$, under the data-availability assumption of Section~\ref{sec:data:grid}; the label does not imply operational validation. Run-now means were 350, 347 and 349 g CO$_2$e per kWh, so the relative savings are 1.9\,\%, 3.8\,\% and 1.5\,\% of run-now emissions. The same planner given perfect information saved more, and $D$ kept 0.33, 0.53 and 0.38 of that saving. Replacing the requested walltime by a prediction from the user's earlier jobs raised the saving on all three traces. The pre-registered test of the deployable saving is confirmatory on PM100 and a secondary replication on the other two traces. Four limits frame these numbers; the first is that the replay is a counterfactual, because jobs from non-U.S. systems run on U.S. signals at their local clock time. The signal is production-average intensity, so the savings are not avoided marginal emissions. The oracle is the same heuristic with perfect inputs, not a global optimum. The simulation has no queue, so the results are bounds under free capacity, and no new scheduler is proposed. Figure~\ref{fig:design} shows what the deployed and the perfect-information scheduler each see.

We use the following terms throughout: run-now is the carbon-agnostic baseline that starts every job at arrival. The slack $S$ is the maximum delay of a job's start. The requested walltime is the limit that the user supplies at submission and that the scheduler enforces by killing the job (state TIMEOUT); the actual runtime is the measured run time. In the planner, each job is started at the time within its slack that minimizes the integral of carbon intensity over the job's estimated duration. The perfect-information arm, called the oracle, is the same planner given the realized intensity and, in one cell of the design, the actual runtime. A policy is called deployable here if it uses only what is known at submission, under the data-availability assumption of Section~\ref{sec:data:grid} that the intensity of the hour just ended is available at arrival; the label does not claim a field test or operational validation. Carbon intensity is the average, production-based, location-based intensity of a balancing authority (BA), an entity that balances supply and demand on a section of the U.S. grid and reports to the U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA). Marginal intensity, the quantity relevant to a change in demand, is not modeled; the savings are savings in accounted average intensity, and the choice of signal can change them \cite{sukprasert2024avgmarg}.

This paper makes four contributions.
\begin{itemize}
  \item A pre-registered measurement, on real HPC batch-job traces and 42 U.S. BAs with a held-out year, of the share of the perfect-information carbon saving that delay scheduling realizes with a past-only forecast and the requested walltime, reported in absolute and relative terms.
  \item A $2\times 2$ design (signal by runtime estimate) with a descriptive Shapley allocation that divides the gap between the perfect-information and the deployable arm into a forecast term and a runtime term, together with the saving recovered by a history-based runtime prediction.
  \item Calendar and synchronized-week intervals for every estimate, an exploratory check of the added uncertainty from the workload sample, and a labeled block of robustness checks, with small and negative results reported as found.
  \item The archiving of code, derived data, the result ledger, the pre-registration and its checksum on Zenodo, with the source repository private until publication.
\end{itemize}

Section~\ref{sec:related} places the study among earlier work. Section~\ref{sec:data} describes the carbon-intensity data and the job traces, and Section~\ref{sec:method} defines the arms, the estimands, the forecaster and the inference. Section~\ref{sec:results} reports the results, Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses them together with the limitations and threats to validity, and Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.


===== 02_related =====
% 02_related.tmpl.tex: SPEC s5 "2 Related work" (850 words). Tables: T1 comparison table (tab:related).
\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}
% 2.1 Temporal shifting and carbon-aware computing (300 words)
\subsection{Temporal shifting and carbon-aware computing}\label{sec:rel_shifting}

Wiesner et al.\ \cite{wiesner2021} delay nightly and machine-learning jobs against the carbon intensity of four regions in 2020, compare an optimal signal with forecasts that carry injected error, and take the delay tolerance as given. Sukprasert et al.\ \cite{sukprasert2024limits} extend the evaluation to 123 regions with synthetic job classes that differ in length, deadline and interruptibility, report ideal upper bounds beside simple and sophisticated policies, and include a prediction-error scenario. Because that study compares policies of different sophistication, the present study keeps a night rule and a persistence rule beside the planner. Radovanovi\'c et al.\ \cite{radovanovic2023} report a production system that shifts flexible load of a large cloud fleet using day-ahead carbon forecasts. The system was measured in deployment and aimed at cloud load rather than HPC batch jobs.

CarbonCast \cite{maji2022carboncast} forecasts grid carbon intensity several days ahead from source-level forecasts. It is a forecasting method, not a savings study, and the present study instead trains a simpler forecaster on past data only. Further work widens the decision space or the objective. Hanafy et al.\ price the cost of reducing carbon \cite{hanafy2024green}, CarbonScaler scales elastic workloads \cite{hanafy2023carbonscaler}, and CASPER places services across sites and time \cite{souza2023casper}. Cucumber admits delay-tolerant jobs under renewable supply \cite{cucumber2022}, and Lechowicz et al.\ schedule dependent batch jobs in data-processing clusters \cite{lechowicz2025}. Hall et al.\ treat forecast uncertainty in data center control \cite{hall2025}, Lin and Chien modulate data center capacity \cite{linchien2023}, and Asadov et al.\ review spatio-temporal shifting \cite{asadov2025}. The jobs studied here are rigid and non-interruptible, so only the start time is a decision.

\subsection{Closest papers in this journal}\label{sec:rel_suscom}

Attenni et al.\ \cite{suscom_101498} simulate spatial, temporal and spatio-temporal shifting of cloud workloads over carbon, water and land use. Their workloads are Azure Functions and Spark traces with synthetic arrival patterns, and the forecast is a noise-perturbed grid mix. They state that job runtimes are assumed known to the scheduler and that a trained operational forecaster remains future work; these are the two inputs measured here. Zhai et al.\ \cite{suscom_101216} schedule delay-tolerant aggregate workload over three wind-powered data centers, forecast workload and wind power with decomposition-LSTM models, and vary injected wind-forecast error. The workload has no per-job runtimes, and the signal is wind generation, not grid carbon intensity.

The ReBase study \cite{suscom_101470} compares a forecast-driven mixed-integer baseline with learned correction against an oracle that uses realized wind and workload. It reports the wind-utilization gap of its own method to the oracle by forecast horizon, from 1.03 percent at the shortest horizon to 11.33 percent at the longest. Its tasks are unit tasks of equal energy, so no job runtime enters, and the gap is attributed to forecast error alone. The forecast-to-oracle gap is therefore a known quantity; the present study adds a trained past-only forecaster on grid carbon intensity, real jobs and a second information factor. Liow et al.\ \cite{suscom_101407} select a region and start time from marginal-emissions forecasts for emulated tasks of three lengths under a 12 h latency bound. Their interval is over three task profiles, and forecast error is named as a limitation. Chen et al.\ \cite{suscom_100950} schedule the offline load of a cloud trace across Chinese data centers with static regional emission factors and without forecasts or runtime estimates.

Reviews and neighbors appear in one clause each: a systematic review of green-aware management \cite{suscom_100989}, carbon-aware federated orchestration in edge-cloud systems \cite{suscom_101398}, carbon-conscious management of data centers with photovoltaic and battery storage \cite{suscom_101429}, and power and energy models for HPC applications \cite{suscom_101106}.

% 2.3 Runtime estimates, prediction in HPC and the closest carbon-aware works (200 words)
\subsection{Runtime estimates and the closest carbon-aware works}\label{sec:rel_runtime}

A requested walltime is an upper bound that users set above the expected runtime. Mu'alem and Feitelson \cite{mualem2001} find user estimates on three IBM SP2 logs to be rather poor but a good upper bound, because few jobs are killed at their limit. They note that inaccurate estimates need not harm a backfilling scheduler, so the requested-walltime arm here is a loss of planning information, not a defect of the scheduler. Tsafrir et al.\ \cite{tsafrir2007} keep user estimates only as kill times and schedule with the mean runtime of the user's last two jobs. This raises average accuracy, the mean over jobs of runtime divided by the estimate, from between 31 and 47 percent to about 60 to 62 percent on four logs. Machine-learning predictors \cite{gaussier2015}, the penalty of underestimates that kill jobs \cite{fan2017}, and a trace with refined runtime estimates \cite{klusacek2024} extend this line; a status report on parallel job scheduling \cite{feitelson2004} gives the background. These works target waiting time and utilization, not carbon.

Three works outside this journal bound the runtime and real-trace claims. LACS \cite{bostandoost2024} scales elastic jobs of unknown length with a learning-augmented algorithm, using one grid, synthetic job lengths, simulated forecast error and simulated length-prediction error. COAST \cite{feng2026}, an arXiv preprint, recommends start times for HPC jobs on a congestion game, using a public HPC log and one grid with actual runtimes and no forecast. LiteCast \cite{joseph2025}, also a preprint, builds a lightweight forecaster for 50 regions using weather and demand inputs, and replays the KIT FH2 log with job lengths known. Its heuristic saves about 2 percent below the oracle there.

\subsection{Position}\label{sec:rel_position}

Table~\ref{tab:related} sets these studies side by side. The forecast-to-oracle gap is known, and the planner here is the delay-to-lowest-integral policy of Wiesner et al.\ and Sukprasert et al.; no new scheduler is proposed. None of the five papers from this journal uses a user-requested walltime or splits the gap between two inputs. Among the three outside works compared above, we did not find an evaluation that combines a past-only carbon forecast, requested walltimes and real HPC traces; this statement is limited to the papers reviewed. The present study contributes that measurement.

\tabfile{tables/T1_related.tex}{T1 comparison of the closest studies}


