\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

This section states the planner, the arms, the runtime estimates, the estimands, the inference and the registration in enough detail to rebuild them. Parts that were added after the freeze are labeled post-freeze.

\subsection{Planner and realized emissions}\label{sec:method:planner}

A job has arrival time $a$ (hours), requested walltime $R_{\mathrm{req}}$, actual runtime $R_{\mathrm{act}}$ and mean power $P$, and it cannot be interrupted. A slack $S\in\{2,6,12,24\}$ h is the longest delay a scheduler may impose; 12 h is the primary value. Candidate starts lie on a 15-min grid,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}(a,S)=\{a,\;a+\Delta,\;a+2\Delta,\;\dots,\;a+S\},\qquad \Delta=\tfrac14\ \mathrm{h}.
\label{eq:starts}
\end{equation}
The planner sees a signal $\hat c_a(t)$, piecewise constant on 98 hourly intervals that begin at $\lfloor a\rfloor$. This width covers the longest plan and realization window, $\mathrm{frac}(a)+S+R_{\mathrm{req}}\le 1+24+72<98$ h, for every signal alike, so that arms differ only in the signal or the runtime estimate.

The planner believes that the job runs $\hat R$ hours and chooses the start that minimizes the integral of its signal over the believed run, with the earliest start winning ties,
\begin{equation}
s^{\star}=\min\Bigl(\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{s\in\mathcal{S}(a,S)}\int_{s}^{s+\hat R}\hat c_{a}(t)\,\mathrm{d}t\Bigr).
\label{eq:planner}
\end{equation}

A window that reaches past the planner's 98 intervals is cut at the edge. Realized emissions are then scored on the actual intensity $c(t)$ over the true run,
\begin{equation}
E=\frac{P}{1000}\int_{s^{\star}}^{s^{\star}+R_{\mathrm{act}}}c(t)\,\mathrm{d}t,
\label{eq:realised}
\end{equation}
which is in g CO$_2$e when $P$ is in W, $c$ in g CO$_2$e per kWh and $t$ in h. Any job whose 98-interval window touches an unusable grid hour is excluded.

This is the delay-to-lowest-integral policy of earlier temporal-shifting studies \citep{wiesner2021,sukprasert2024limits}. No new scheduler is proposed, and no other published one is reimplemented. The oracle is this heuristic with perfect inputs, $\hat c_a=c$ and $\hat R=R_{\mathrm{act}}$; it is not a global optimum. The replay has no queue and no capacity limit, so every saving is a bound under free capacity. Algorithm~\ref{alg:planner} gives the planner together with the runtime estimate of Section~\ref{sec:method:runtime}.

[algorithm omitted]

\subsection{Arms and the two-by-two design}\label{sec:method:arms}

An arm pairs a signal with a runtime estimate and is written \emph{signal|estimate}. The signals are the oracle (actual intensity), persistence, seasonal naive day, seasonal naive week, profile7 (the mean of the last seven same-hour values), the LightGBM forecaster, and a historical profile (mean TRAIN intensity by month and local hour). The runtime estimates are act, req, pred and pred2 (Section~\ref{sec:method:runtime}). Two baselines need no signal: run-now (start at $a$) and a night rule (the earliest candidate start between 00:00 and 03:59 local time, else now).

The deployable arm $D$ is lgbm|req, a forecast-and-request policy under the data-availability assumption of Section~\ref{sec:data:grid}: it uses only data from before the arrival and the information a user gives at submission. The label does not imply operational validation. It was selected on DEV, before the test year was scored, because the final forecaster beat the seasonal naive day on 1 to 24 h error in most BAs (Section~\ref{sec:method:forecast}). The four corners oracle|act, oracle|req, lgbm|act and lgbm|req form a two-by-two design: oracle versus forecast changes the signal, and act versus req changes the runtime estimate.

The remaining arms are secondary, and four exploratory arms were added after the freeze. The first is a forecast-free threshold rule that starts at the first quarter hour whose observed intensity lies below its trailing 7-day 25th percentile (else at $a+S$). The second is a padded estimate $\min(R_{\mathrm{req}},\tfrac32\hat R_{\mathrm{pred}})$, and the third is a quantile estimate (the user's 90th percentile of past runtimes, from at least five jobs, capped at $R_{\mathrm{req}}$, else $R_{\mathrm{req}}$). The fourth is a sweep $\hat R=mR_{\mathrm{act}}$ with $m\in\{\tfrac14,\tfrac12,1,2,4,8\}$ (X1). Table~\ref{tab:arms} lists arms and estimands, and Figure~\ref{fig:design} shows the design.

\subsection{Forecasters}\label{sec:method:forecast}

The LightGBM forecaster is one global gradient-boosted model across BAs \citep{friedman2001}, with the forecast lead as a feature. It predicts the difference between actual intensity and the profile7 value, under an absolute-error objective. Its inputs are intensity lags and trailing means and deviations, calendar features of the target hour, the three simple forecasts for the target hour, the fuel shares at the issue hour, and a BA code. It issues leads 1 to 96 at arrival $a$ from data up to $\lfloor a\rfloor$, and the planner holds the lead-96 value for the last two intervals,
\begin{equation}
\hat c_{a}(k)=\begin{cases}f_{\theta}\bigl(x_{\le\lfloor a\rfloor},\,k+1\bigr), & k=0,\dots,95,\\[2pt] \hat c_{a}(95), & k=96,97,\end{cases}
\label{eq:fclead}
\end{equation}
where $f_\theta$ is the forecaster trained on TRAIN only and $x_{\le\lfloor a\rfloor}$ are causal inputs. Early stopping uses the last six months of TRAIN, with a purged boundary so that no training target falls in the validation window. The model is never refitted on DEV or TEST, and its hyperparameters were fixed before the test year (a tuned variant, S5, is exploratory). Inputs repaired by interpolation or outlier replacement are replaced by the last earlier unflagged value, so repairs cannot leak the future. A future-perturbation test replaced every value after the issue hour by noise at randomly chosen issue hours: 150 tests, of which 0 failed.

On DEV, the forecaster had lower 1 to 24 h mean absolute error than the seasonal naive day in 38 of 42 BAs and lower than profile7 in 41 of them. The basis for choosing $D$ was this comparison and not any test result. A lower error does not imply a higher saving, since the planner uses only the ranking of intensities inside a window, so saving is reported beside error rather than inferred from it. The forecaster is a simple, grid-only model and not a replication of weather-driven systems such as CarbonCast \citep{maji2022carboncast}. Error measures follow common practice \citep{hyndman2006}, with time-ordered validation \citep{cerqueira2020}.

\subsection{Runtime estimates}\label{sec:method:runtime}

The estimate act uses $R_{\mathrm{act}}$ and is an oracle input. The estimate req uses $R_{\mathrm{req}}$, which a scheduler knows at submission. The estimate pred follows the history-based predictors of backfilling research \citep{tsafrir2007,gaussier2015,fan2017}. It is the mean actual runtime of the user's two most recent jobs that ended before this job was submitted in state COMPLETED or TIMEOUT, capped at the request,
\begin{equation}
\hat R_{\mathrm{pred}}=\min\Bigl(R_{\mathrm{req}},\ \tfrac12\bigl(R_{(1)}+R_{(2)}\bigr)\Bigr),\qquad
\hat R_{\mathrm{pred2}}=\min\bigl(R_{\mathrm{req}},\,2\hat R_{\mathrm{pred}}\bigr).
\label{eq:rhat}
\end{equation}

With one earlier job, $\hat R_{\mathrm{pred}}$ is that job's runtime capped at the request, and with none it is $R_{\mathrm{req}}$. The history comes from the user's real past in the original trace, so replay in a new week does not change it. A naive filter recomputed 1,000 random jobs per trace, and an assertion that every history job ended before the submission passed on all three traces. Because pred is a deliberately simple predictor, the recovery it shows may understate what a better predictor could achieve; the replay does not test this. The factor 2 in pred2 was fixed before any test result and not tuned.

\subsection{Job sampling and calendar alignment}\label{sec:method:align}

For each trace, BA and alignment replicate $r$ we draw without replacement 20{,}000 jobs from the filtered trace, or all jobs if fewer. The generator is seeded from the base seed 20261111, $r$ and the BA and trace names. Each drawn job is placed in an independently drawn whole week of the period, at the weekday and clock time of its original submission. Clock time is read in the BA's standard local time (a fixed offset, with no daylight saving shift). Each BA and trace has 3 replicates. We drop jobs whose window touches an unusable hour. Outcomes are summed per arrival day and per runtime class, and the replicates are added on a common calendar-day index. The main outcome weights each job by its energy. An unweighted variant gives each job one weight,
\begin{equation}
\bar c_{\mathrm{u}}=\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^{n}\frac{1}{R_{\mathrm{act},j}}\int_{s_j^{\star}}^{s_j^{\star}+R_{\mathrm{act},j}}c(t)\,\mathrm{d}t,
\label{eq:unweighted}
\end{equation}
the mean intensity a job experiences; it is as if every job used one kWh. Algorithm~\ref{alg:replay} describes one arm, one BA and one replicate.

[algorithm omitted]

\subsection{Estimands and the Shapley split}\label{sec:method:estimands}

The saving of an arm is one minus its emissions over the run-now emissions, pooled over BAs, days, runtime classes and replicates,
\begin{equation}
\sigma(\mathrm{arm})=1-\frac{\sum_{j}E_{j}^{\mathrm{arm}}}{\sum_{j}E_{j}^{\mathrm{run\text{-}now}}}.
\label{eq:saving}
\end{equation}
Because jobs are pooled before the ratio is taken, $\sigma$ is carbon-weighted: BAs with high intensity dominate it, because every BA receives the same number of sampled jobs. The inference target is the fixed set of BAs over the test year. Per-BA medians of the runtime penalty are shown beside the pooled value. The three confirmatory hypotheses, tested on PM100 at $S=12$ h, concern
\begin{equation}
\theta_1=\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|req}),\qquad
\theta_2=\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|act})-\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|req}),\qquad
\theta_3=\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|pred})-\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|req}),
\label{eq:hyp}
\end{equation}
where each hypothesis states $\theta>0$. H1 says that the deployable arm saves emissions, H2 that knowing the actual runtime adds saving (the runtime penalty of using the request), and H3 that the history predictor recovers saving over the request. Differences of savings are in percentage points (pp).

The gap between perfect information and the deployable arm is $G=\sigma(\mathrm{oracle|act})-\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|req})$. We split it over the two information factors with a two-player Shapley allocation \citep{lundberg2020,owen2014}. Writing $o$ and $l$ for the oracle and LightGBM signals and $a$ and $r$ for act and req,
\begin{align}
\phi_F&=\tfrac12\bigl[\sigma(o|a)-\sigma(l|a)+\sigma(o|r)-\sigma(l|r)\bigr],\notag\\
\phi_R&=\tfrac12\bigl[\sigma(o|a)-\sigma(o|r)+\sigma(l|a)-\sigma(l|r)\bigr],\qquad \phi_F+\phi_R=G,
\label{eq:shapley}
\end{align}
and the interaction, which each term shares equally, is
\begin{equation}
\theta_4=\bigl[\sigma(o|a)-\sigma(o|r)\bigr]-\bigl[\sigma(l|a)-\sigma(l|r)\bigr].
\label{eq:interaction}
\end{equation}

The terms are descriptive allocations of an accounting identity and not causal effects, and their order can differ between traces. The interaction $\theta_4$ (H4) was demoted before testing and is descriptive. The retained share is the fraction of the perfect-information saving that the deployable arm keeps,
\begin{equation}
\rho=\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|req})\big/\sigma(\mathrm{oracle|act}),
\label{eq:retained}
\end{equation}
slack-specific and conditional on the workload.

Relative savings hide scale, so absolute effects are also reported. With the job energy $\varepsilon_j=P_jR_{\mathrm{act},j}/1000$ in kWh ($P_j$ in W, $R_{\mathrm{act},j}$ in h) and $\bar c_{\mathrm{now}}=\sum_j E_j^{\mathrm{run\text{-}now}}\big/\sum_j \varepsilon_j$ in g CO$_2$e per kWh of job energy,
\begin{equation}
\Delta_{\mathrm{abs}}=\sigma\,\bar c_{\mathrm{now}},\qquad
\Delta_{\mathrm{job}}=\frac1n\sum_{j}\bigl(E_j^{\mathrm{run\text{-}now}}-E_j^{\mathrm{arm}}\bigr).
\label{eq:abs}
\end{equation}
The absolute effects were added after the freeze (post-hoc) from the saved results. Mean start delay, $\bar d=\frac1n\sum_j(s^{\star}_j-a_j)$, is reported per arm.

\subsection{Inference}\label{sec:method:inference}

Every interval and test uses a block bootstrap on arrival days. For each BA, the per-day sums of Algorithm~\ref{alg:replay} are cut into consecutive 7-day blocks that start at the BA's first arrival day, and a remainder shorter than 7 days is kept fixed. A draw resamples each BA's blocks with replacement, independently across BAs and with all BAs kept, and recomputes every estimand from the pooled sums. With 2{,}000 draws and seed $7+S$, the interval of a statistic $T$ with draws $T^{*}_{k}$ is the percentile interval
\begin{equation}
\bigl[\,T^{*}_{(\alpha/2)},\ T^{*}_{(1-\alpha/2)}\bigr],\qquad \alpha=\tfrac1{20},
\label{eq:interval}
\end{equation}
the empirical quantiles of the draws. It is called the \emph{calendar interval}. It treats the job sample as fixed and describes weather and calendar variation within each BA, with the BAs resampled independently. Independent resampling lets weeks that are unusually clean in one BA be paired with weeks that are dirty in another. It can therefore understate the variation of a pooled estimate that is driven by shared weather.

Two post-freeze intervals complement it and are named in every table and caption. The \emph{synchronized-week interval} (post-hoc, PH1) uses the same week indices for all BAs in each draw, with blocks aligned on the calendar weeks of the common test span (50 blocks),
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}^{*}_{k}=\bigl(w_{k,1},\dots,w_{k,K}\bigr),\qquad w_{k,i}\ \text{shared by every BA},
\label{eq:syncweek}
\end{equation}
with the same seed rule. Pooling over BAs first is exact because the indices are shared; the same code gives an interval for the difference $\phi_R-\phi_F$ and for each Shapley term under both bootstraps.

The \emph{workload-sample interval} is computed in an exploratory check (S1, Section~\ref{sec:results:robust}). It resamples users with replacement, draws a new job sample and alignment from the resampled pool, and then applies the frozen blocks, with 30 outer draws and 200 inner draws each, plus an original-users control. The check reports a normal-approximation total by the law of total variance beside the percentile version,
\begin{equation}
\hat T\pm z_{1-\alpha/2}\sqrt{s^{2}_{\mathrm{cal}}+s^{2}_{\mathrm{outer}}},
\label{eq:total}
\end{equation}
where $\hat T$ is the frozen estimate, $s^2_{\mathrm{cal}}$ the variance of the frozen calendar-bootstrap draws and $s^2_{\mathrm{outer}}$ the variance of the point estimates over the outer draws.

The original-users control (same seeds, users not resampled, one alignment replicate each) shows how much of the outer spread comes from the alignment draw alone. It is reported beside the total and is not subtracted from it, so the total interval is conservative. A variant replaces $s^2_{\mathrm{cal}}$ by the mean inner variance of the outer draws. The target is the variation of the saving over the users of each trace, not over sites or systems. Only the calendar interval enters hypothesis tests. A two-stage bootstrap that resamples BAs first and then blocks is read as uncertainty over a hypothetical population of BAs and is in the supplement. For the per-BA test of H2, the same block bootstrap runs within each BA with 500 draws.

For H1 to H3 the one-sided p-value is null-centered: with estimate $T$ and draws $T^{*}_{k}$,
\begin{equation}
p=\frac{1+\#\{k:\ T^{*}_{k}-T\ge T\}}{B+1},
\label{eq:pvalue}
\end{equation}
so the smallest attainable value is $1/(B+1)$, written p $<$ 0.0005. The three p-values are adjusted by Holm's step-down procedure \citep{holm1979} at level $\alpha$, with $m=3$ and sorted p-values $p_{(1)}\le p_{(2)}\le p_{(3)}$,
\begin{equation}
p^{\mathrm{Holm}}_{(i)}=\min\Bigl\{1,\ \max_{h\le i}(m-h+1)\,p_{(h)}\Bigr\}.
\label{eq:holm}
\end{equation}
% holm1979 cited at the introduction of the Holm correction

A hypothesis counts as supported when its adjusted value is below $\alpha$; the adjusted floor is 0.0015. The frozen code also computes an unadjusted two-sided p-value for the interaction H4, which is not reported; H4 appears with its interval only, and no other quantity gets a p-value. Resampling follows the bootstrap \citep{efron1979} and its block form \citep{kunsch1989}; a stationary variant \citep{politis1994} was not used. Algorithm~\ref{alg:evaluation} gives the whole procedure.

[algorithm omitted]

\subsection{Registration, deviations, analysis registry and reproducibility}\label{sec:method:registry}

The design was registered and frozen on 2026-11-11, before the test year was scored. The simulator refuses the test period unless the md5 sum of the registration (starting cfe98401) matches the stored value, and the sums of the five analysis scripts are recorded. Five entries were logged afterward. The unweighted outcome gives each job equal weight, as if each used 1 kWh, and not 1 kW as the registration says (row 1). The TIMEOUT-included analysis (S4) was pre-registered as secondary but not run at the freeze (row 2). The secondary-analysis code was written after the test run and only reads saved results (row 3), and test arrivals span 2025-07-07 to 2026-06-14 (row 4), and the post-hoc inference below was added (row 5). Nothing in the frozen code or estimates changed.

Everything after the freeze carries a status. Post-hoc items (PH1 to PH7) are descriptive and use only saved results. They are the synchronized-week interval, Shapley-term intervals, per-BA H2 intervals, absolute effects, the paired comparison of lgbm|req with profile7|req, and saving per hour of delay. Secondary, run after the freeze (PREREG item 6; deviation row 2), is the analysis with TIMEOUT jobs included (S4). Exploratory items are the workload-sample interval (S1), data latency (S2 and S2b), accounting factors (S3) and a tuned forecaster (S5). They also include the new arms, the runtime sweep (X1 to X7) and a clock shift of 3 h in either direction. An item that did not run is stated as not run. A post-freeze result is reported beside the frozen value and is never promoted to a confirmatory claim; the analysis registry in the supplement lists every analysis with its status, file and ledger.

Every number in the paper is read from a ledger written by script from saved results. The runs used Python 3.14.3 with numpy 2.4.2, pandas 2.3.3, LightGBM 4.7.0 and, for the tuned forecaster S5, Optuna 5.0.0. The numpy version is part of the seed because its random streams depend on it. LightGBM trains with \texttt{deterministic=True}, seed 42 for every seed parameter and 20 threads.

The package holds an environment lock file, the md5 sum of every result and forecast file, and the commands of each run. The simulator and analysis commands were typed interactively, so the recorded commands were reconstructed from the logs, run files and parser defaults. The script \texttt{rebuild\_ledgers.py} rebuilds both frozen test ledgers from the saved results exactly, which checks the reconstruction \citep{nosek2018}.


