\providecommand{\Arm}[2]{\mbox{#1\textbar{}#2}}
\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

Every estimate is a replay: it states what a job trace would have emitted under each arm on the 42 grids in the test year, and not what a facility emitted. Savings are relative to run-now, pooled over BAs and weighted by energy. Differences of savings are in percentage points (pp), and intervals are 95\,\%. The \emph{calendar interval} is the frozen block bootstrap, and the \emph{synchronized-week interval} is the post-hoc variant that shares week indices across BAs (Section~\ref{sec:method:inference}). Each subsection opens with the status of its content. Only H1 to H3 carry a p-value, and they are confirmatory on PM100 at 12 h slack. Every other result is secondary, post-hoc or exploratory, and slack is $S=12$ h unless stated.

\subsection{Main result at 12 h slack}\label{sec:results:main}

\emph{Confirmatory on PM100 (H1); secondary replications on F-DATA and KIT FH2.} Under run-now, the mean realized intensity of job energy is 350 g CO$_2$e per kWh on PM100 (calendar interval 347 to 353), 347 on F-DATA and 349 on KIT FH2 (Table~\ref{tab:confirm}, Panel A). The three values are close because every trace is replayed on the same grids in the same year, so savings per kWh are the better cross-trace comparison. The deployable arm \Arm{lgbm}{req} ($D$) removes 6.6 g CO$_2$e per kWh on PM100 (calendar interval 6.3 to 7.0), which is 47 g CO$_2$e per job (45 to 49). It removes 13.2 g per kWh on F-DATA and 5.3 on KIT FH2. The perfect-information arm \Arm{oracle}{act} removes 20.1 g per kWh on PM100, 25.0 on F-DATA and 14.0 on KIT FH2. Per-job values differ across traces because jobs differ in energy (Table~\ref{tab:confirm}).

In relative terms, $D$ saves 1.90\,\% of run-now emissions on PM100 (calendar interval 1.81\,\% to 1.99\,\%; synchronized-week interval 1.73\,\% to 2.05\,\%), and H1 is supported ($p<0.0005$, Holm-adjusted $<0.0015$). The perfect-information arm saves 5.75\,\% (calendar interval 5.64\,\% to 5.87\,\%), so $D$ keeps 33.0\,\% of it (calendar interval 31.7\,\% to 34.2\,\%; synchronized-week interval 31.1\,\% to 34.6\,\%). This retained share is pooled, carbon-weighted and conditional on this workload and slack, and it is not a population range. A start shift of at most 12 h can reach only hours within half a day of arrival. The planner sees a past-only forecast of those hours (its error is in Figure~\ref{fig:forecast}) and a requested walltime that is long for most jobs (among the analyzed jobs the median ratio of actual to requested runtime is 0.14). Both would limit what the replay can recover. Figure~\ref{fig:savings} shows all arms on the three traces.

The baseline ladder is in Table~\ref{tab:confirm}, Panel D. On PM100 the night rule saves 0.29\,\%, and persistence with the requested walltime saves $-$0.20\,\%, a loss. Seasonal naive day and week save 0.96\,\% and 1.16\,\%, profile7 1.11\,\% and the historical profile 0.73\,\%, all with the requested walltime. On every trace and at every slack, $D$ saves more than the night rule and more than each of these past-only signals paired with the requested walltime. Among the signals and arms tested here, at the slacks and on the traces studied, the trained forecaster therefore gives the highest saving under the requested walltime.

\subsection{Decomposition of the gap and history prediction}\label{sec:results:decomp}

\emph{Secondary (forecast skill), post-hoc and descriptive (decomposition), confirmatory on PM100 (H3).} Forecast skill is read before the split because it sets the forecast term. On the test year, the median over BAs of the mean absolute error at leads of 1 to 24 h is 22.0 g/kWh for LightGBM, against 24.0 for seasonal naive day, 27.8 for profile7 and 42.3 for persistence. LightGBM has a lower error than seasonal naive day in 37 of 42 BAs. At leads of 49 to 96 h the medians are 30.6 and 35.4 g/kWh for LightGBM and seasonal naive day (Figure~\ref{fig:forecast}). Error alone does not say whether the forecaster matters for the saving. Replacing it by profile7 under the requested walltime lowers the saving by 0.79\,pp on PM100 (calendar interval 0.71\,pp to 0.87\,pp; synchronized-week interval 0.66\,pp to 0.90\,pp), by 0.47\,pp on F-DATA and by 0.53\,pp on KIT FH2. All three intervals exclude zero under both bootstraps. This is a descriptive comparison of the two forecast profiles on the replayed workload, not a confirmatory test, and it suggests that the trained forecaster adds saving beyond a profile of recent hours.

The saving lost between \Arm{oracle}{act} and $D$ is the gap $G$, 3.86\,pp on PM100. The four corner savings are 5.75\,\%, 3.33\,\%, 4.08\,\% and 1.90\,\% for \Arm{oracle}{act}, \Arm{oracle}{req}, \Arm{lgbm}{act} and \Arm{lgbm}{req}. The Shapley split allocates 1.56\,pp to the forecast term $\phi_F$ (calendar interval 1.50\,pp to 1.61\,pp) and 2.30\,pp to the runtime term $\phi_R$ (2.24\,pp to 2.37\,pp). The runtime term is larger than the forecast term on PM100: the interval of $\phi_R-\phi_F$ is 0.67\,pp to 0.83\,pp (calendar) and 0.62\,pp to 0.88\,pp (synchronized-week), and the runtime term is 59.7\,\% of the gap. The ordering is the same on KIT FH2 (difference 0.45\,pp, calendar interval 0.42\,pp to 0.49\,pp). On F-DATA the two terms are comparable: the difference is $-$0.11\,pp, and its calendar interval $-$0.29\,pp to 0.08\,pp and its synchronized-week interval $-$0.31\,pp to 0.10\,pp both include zero (Figure~\ref{fig:shapley}). The interaction $\theta_4$ is 0.24\,pp on PM100 (calendar interval 0.18\,pp to 0.30\,pp). Because it is not zero, the size of each term depends on the symmetric allocation convention. The terms are descriptive allocations of an accounting identity and not causal effects.

History prediction recovers part of the runtime term. The prediction recovery H3 is 1.03\,pp on PM100 (calendar interval 0.96\,pp to 1.10\,pp; synchronized-week interval 0.93\,pp to 1.13\,pp), and H3 is supported ($p<0.0005$, Holm-adjusted $<0.0015$). The arm \Arm{lgbm}{pred} saves 2.93\,\% and keeps 50.9\,\% of the perfect-information saving, while \Arm{lgbm}{pred2} saves 2.37\,\%. \Arm{lgbm}{pred} is the best registered arm that uses neither the actual runtime nor the realized intensity on every trace, so its margin over $D$ equals H3 (1.03\,pp on PM100). A prediction from the user's past jobs narrows the distance between the believed and the actual runtime, which is the likely route to the recovery; the replay does not test the route directly.

\subsection{Slack}\label{sec:results:slack}

\emph{Secondary and descriptive.} Saving rises with the slack for the deployable arm and for the perfect-information arm (Figure~\ref{fig:slack}a). On PM100, $D$ saves 0.26\,\% at 2 h, 0.79\,\% at 6 h, 1.90\,\% at 12 h and 2.68\,\% at 24 h, against 1.05\,\%, 2.98\,\%, 5.75\,\% and 8.62\,\% for \Arm{oracle}{act}. The deployable arm stays below the perfect-information arm at every slack on every trace. The absolute gap between the two widens with the slack, and the retained share moves less regularly, at 25\,\%, 26\,\%, 33\,\% and 31\,\%. The retained share has no meaning independent of the slack, so it is read together with the absolute savings. A longer window gives the planner more variation to use and more forecast lead to get wrong, which may explain why the share stops rising between 12 h and 24 h. The history-based arm \Arm{lgbm}{pred} saves 0.42\,\% at 2 h and 4.26\,\% at 24 h.

\subsection{Heterogeneity across balancing authorities and job length}\label{sec:results:hetero}

\emph{Post-hoc intervals for the per-BA counts; descriptive associations with no p-values.} The runtime penalty H2 is positive in 41 of 42 BAs on PM100, and the lower bound of its within-BA 95\,\% interval is above zero in 41 of them. The median per-BA penalty is 1.35\,pp. On F-DATA the penalty is positive in 40 BAs and its lower bound is above zero in 33, with a smallest lower bound of $-$7.0\,pp. On KIT FH2 the counts are 41 and 40 (Figure~\ref{fig:ba}). The within-BA intervals use 50 weekly blocks and 500 draws, so they are wide and are shown as intervals and not as tests. One BA, SPA, has a constant intensity in the test year, so its oracle saving is zero and its retained share is undefined.

Across the 42 BAs, the perfect-information saving on PM100 rises with the median daily range of intensity (Spearman $\rho=$ 0.82), and the runtime penalty rises with the solar share of generation ($\rho=$ 0.87). The corresponding values are 0.81 and 0.83 on F-DATA and 0.80 and 0.86 on KIT FH2. The correlations are unadjusted, computed over the fixed set of BAs, and solar and wind shares are tied at zero in many BAs. All eight BA-character correlations are reported in the supplement (Table~\ref{tabS:spearman}). A grid with a large daily swing offers more to a shift, and a solar-heavy grid has a sharp midday minimum that a wrong runtime estimate can miss; the replay does not test either reading.

By job length, the perfect-information saving on PM100 falls from 10.6\,\% for jobs under 1 h to 3.9\,\% for jobs of 12 h or more. The deployable arm keeps 38\,\% of the saving in the shortest class, 36\,\% and 39\,\% in the two middle classes and 27\,\% in the longest, which holds 201{,}206 of the 2{,}520{,}000 replayed jobs (Figure~\ref{fig:explain}). The shares are pooled and descriptive, with no interval. Long jobs span many hours and average over the variation that a shift exploits, which is a plausible reason for their small saving.

\subsection{Secondary traces as replications}\label{sec:results:replic}

\emph{Secondary; replications in direction, never confirmations.} On both other traces the three contrasts have the sign found on PM100 and their calendar intervals exclude zero (Table~\ref{tab:confirm}, Panel B); these are secondary replications, not confirmations. On F-DATA, $D$ saves 3.81\,\%, the runtime penalty is 1.50\,pp and the prediction recovery 0.53\,pp. On KIT FH2 the values are 1.51\,\%, 1.31\,pp and 0.35\,pp. The registered tests cover PM100 only, so these are replications of direction and size and carry no p-value. The retained share is 52.8\,\% on F-DATA (calendar interval 49.7\,\% to 55.5\,\%) and 37.6\,\% on KIT FH2 (36.9\,\% to 38.2\,\%), against 33.0\,\% on PM100 (Table~\ref{tab:secondary}). The runtime term exceeds the forecast term on KIT FH2 as on PM100, and not on F-DATA (Section~\ref{sec:results:decomp}). F-DATA has the tightest requests, with a median actual-to-requested ratio of 0.31 against 0.14 on PM100 and 0.18 on KIT FH2. With three traces, that ordering is an observation and not a test of why the runtime term differs. KIT FH2 carries an assumed 1 kW for every job, so its energy weights follow runtime only (Section~\ref{sec:data:jobs}).

\subsection{Delay cost}\label{sec:results:delay}

\emph{Secondary (delay) and post-hoc (saving per hour of delay).} Shifting starts costs waiting time. On PM100 at 12 h slack, $D$ delays jobs by 5.5 h on average for its 1.90\,\% saving, which is 0.35\,pp per hour of added delay. The perfect-information arm waits 5.4 h and saves 1.07\,pp per hour. The night rule waits 4.4 h for 0.29\,\%, or 0.06\,pp per hour. The mean delay of $D$ on F-DATA and KIT FH2 is 5.8 h and 5.4 h. At the other slacks it is 0.9 h at 2 h, 2.6 h at 6 h and 12.3 h at 24 h (Figure~\ref{fig:slack}b). The deployable arm thus waits about as long as the perfect-information arm and realizes a smaller saving per hour of waiting, as expected when the window is chosen with imperfect inputs.
\subsection{Robustness}\label{sec:results:robust}

\emph{Frozen variants are secondary; the synchronized-week interval is post-hoc; S1 to S3, X1 and X4 are exploratory and post-freeze, and S4 is secondary, run after the freeze (PREREG item 6; deviation row 2). Cells that depend on queued runs are marked pending in Table~\ref{tab:robust}.} Synchronizing the weeks widens the interval of $D$ by a factor of 1.71 on PM100 and 2.28 on KIT FH2, and leaves it about as wide on F-DATA (0.93). The intervals of H1 to H3 still exclude zero on every trace. The two-stage bootstrap, which resamples BAs and then blocks, gives an interval of 1.45\,\% to 2.39\,\% for $D$ on PM100, which also excludes zero. The calendar and synchronized-week intervals describe weather and calendar variation over the fixed BAs, the two-stage interval adds variation across BAs, and none of them describes the job sample. The exploratory resampling of users (S1) estimates the added workload-sample uncertainty: the standard deviation of $D$ across the outer samples is \pendingkey{}, against \pendingkey{} in the original-users control, the total-variance interval is \pendingkey{} to \pendingkey{}, and the lowest of the outer samples is \pendingkey{} (Figure~\ref{fig:robust}). The exploratory sweep of the runtime-estimate error (X1, below) is plotted in Figure~\ref{fig:runtimex}.

The frozen variants change the size of the saving and not its direction (Figure~\ref{fig:frozen}). Excluding jobs that ran near their limit raises $D$ on PM100 to 2.10\,\%, weighting jobs equally raises it to 3.54\,\%, and the development year gives 1.73\,\%. H2 and H3 stay positive in every variant on all three traces. Both exclusion and equal weighting raise all three contrasts, and the development year gives lower values than the test year on every trace. Equal weighting counts short jobs as much as long ones, which is consistent with the larger saving of short jobs in Section~\ref{sec:results:hetero}.

Data latency (S2) tests how far the planner's information can age. With the intensity delayed by 1, 3 and 6 h, $D$ moves from 1.90\,\% to 1.75\,\%, 1.63\,\% and 1.57\,\%. Seasonal naive day with the requested walltime moves from 0.96\,\% to 0.98\,\%, 0.98\,\% and 1.00\,\%. At 24 and 48 h (S2b) $D$ is 1.33\,\% and 1.30\,\%, which spans the publication delay described in Section~\ref{sec:data:grid}. The series holds revised values, so S2 isolates delay and not revision. Refitting the carbon intensity with direct combustion factors (S3a) gives 2.04\,\% for $D$, and a higher oil factor (S3b) gives 1.90\,\%. Including jobs that hit their limit (S4) gives 2.04\,\% on PM100. Tuning the LightGBM forecaster with a 100-trial search on the training years only (S5, exploratory; Section~\ref{sec:S:s5}) gives 1.96\,\% for $D$ on PM100, against 1.90\,\% with the frozen forecaster.

\emph{X1, exploratory.} The runtime-estimate sweep scales the actual runtime by a factor $m$ (Figure~\ref{fig:runtimex}). For the LightGBM signal, the saving is 2.33\,\% at $m=\tfrac14$, 4.08\,\% at $m=1$ and 1.92\,\% at $m=8$. The requested walltime has a root-mean-square log error of \pendingkey{} against \pendingkey{} for the history prediction. Two simple operator rules are placed beside them: a padded estimate (error \pendingkey{}) saves \pendingkey{} and a quantile estimate (error \pendingkey{}) saves \pendingkey{}, against 1.90\,\% for the request and 2.93\,\% for the prediction.

\emph{X4, exploratory.} Shifting starts toward cleaner hours can concentrate load. On PM100, the peak-to-mean ratio of hourly job power under $D$ is \pendingkey{} times that under run-now, and the 99th percentile of hourly power is \pendingkey{} times; for \Arm{oracle}{act} the factors are \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{} (Figure~\ref{fig:frozen}). This proxy is computed from start times with no queue, so it cannot show whether a shifted load fits the capacity of a system.

Further exploratory checks are reported in the supplement with their status. They cover a tuned forecaster, injected forecast noise, a cap on the forecast lead, concentration of the runtime penalty, the timing of the saving, a fossil-only signal, three new arms and a clock shift of the arrivals. % A27: after S5, X2, X3, X5-X7, the new arms and the shift check have run, replace this sentence by one that names any result that changes a conclusion above; otherwise keep it.


===== 06_discussion =====
% 06_discussion.tmpl.tex: SPEC s5 "6 Discussion" (1,600 words in total). Written by A28.
% Numbers enter only as <<key|fmt>>; <<PENDING:key|fmt>> marks keys that queued sensitivity runs will write (names from src/sens_*.py).
\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{What the $2\times 2$ describes}\label{sec:disc:twobytwo}

The design separates two inputs that a deployed scheduler lacks: the realized carbon signal and the actual runtime. Giving the planner perfect intensity but only the requested walltime (oracle|req) saves 3.33\,\%, 5.42\,\% and 2.37\,\% of run-now emissions on PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2, against 5.75\,\%, 7.22\,\% and 4.02\,\% with the actual runtime as well. This walltime-only cost parallels the forecast-only gap that earlier work reports for an oracle against a forecast-driven scheduler \cite{suscom_101470}.

The Shapley allocation (Eq.~\eqref{eq:shapley}) assigns the remaining gap of D to a forecast term and a runtime term. The forecast term is 1.56\,pp, 1.76\,pp and 1.03\,pp, and the runtime term is 2.30\,pp, 1.65\,pp and 1.48\,pp. The difference between them has a calendar interval and a synchronized-week interval that both exclude zero on PM100 and KIT FH2, where the runtime term is larger, and both include zero on F-DATA, where the terms are comparable. An ordering is therefore trace-specific, and we state none for the three traces together.

The runtime term is consistent with loose requests: the median analyzed job ran for 14.3\,\%, 30.6\,\% and 17.8\,\% of its requested walltime, so a planner that trusts the request reserves a window far longer than the job needs. The interaction is not zero (0.24\,pp, 0.29\,pp and 0.34\,pp), so the two losses do not add. The allocation is descriptive; it does not measure the causal effect of improving either input.

\subsection{Implications as hypotheses}\label{sec:disc:implications}

The replay suggests several hypotheses for future evaluation. For operators, logging the requested and the actual runtime and adding a history-based predictor to a delay planner could be worth testing in a production scheduler, because the prediction raised the saving by 1.03\,pp, 0.53\,pp and 0.35\,pp in the replay. The predictor is deliberately simple, so these values may understate what job history can recover.

In this replay, planning with the requested rather than the actual runtime reduced the modeled saving by 2.18\,pp, 1.50\,pp and 1.31\,pp on PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2. The replay does not test whether a change in the limits that users set would change emissions, and a limit below the true runtime kills the job.

In the replay, the forecast and the runtime estimate act as separate inputs, and with the requested walltime the learned forecaster saved more than the seven-day profile on all three traces, by 0.79\,pp, 0.47\,pp and 0.53\,pp. The planner is the delay-to-lowest-integral policy of earlier work and the comparison covers only the arms tested, so nothing here ranks production schedulers.

For providers of carbon signals, data latency may matter. When the forecast information is delayed by 6 h, the deployable saving on PM100 is 1.57\,\% of run-now emissions, to be read beside the frozen 1.90\,\%.

The absolute effect is modest. D saves 47, 126 and 27 g CO$_2$e per job as a mean over jobs in the pooled replay, and the replay says nothing about the saving a specific site would see.

\subsection{Comparison with earlier work}\label{sec:disc:compare}

A slack of 12 h on a single grid may give smaller savings than studies that move work across regions or follow wind output, because a delay can only move a job within the variation of one grid's average intensity. We draw no numeric comparison with their headline figures. Attenni et al.\ assume known runtimes and a noise-perturbed grid mix \cite{suscom_101498}, and their remark that runtimes could be estimated from previous runs is the position that the prediction arm tests here. LiteCast replays the same KIT FH2 log with a lightweight forecaster, but with actual job lengths and the recorded wait as slack \cite{joseph2025}, so its savings and ours are not comparable. The released code and result ledger let a reader repeat any cell of these comparisons.

\subsection{Limitations and threats to validity}\label{sec:disc:threats}

Each paragraph names one threat, says why the result may still hold, and points to the analysis that addresses it. Analyses run after the freeze are labeled post-hoc or exploratory throughout.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{A. Counterfactual replay on foreign traces.} Marconi100 (2020), Fugaku and KIT ForHLR II (2016 to 2018) are placed on U.S. production signals of the test year, and a U.S. trace such as NLR Kestrel could not be obtained. The agreement of three foreign traces does not establish U.S. behavior, and a balancing authority's standard time set against trace daylight saving can shift summer arrivals by an hour. The study is therefore a what-if replay, not a deployment forecast. A placement-shift check (exploratory, PM100) moves arrivals by 3 h in either direction; the saving of D changes by \pendingkey{} and \pendingkey{} percentage points for the earlier and the later shift (Section~\ref{sec:results}).

\medskip\noindent\textbf{B. Average, not marginal, intensity.} The signal is production-average intensity from EIA-930; imports and storage are excluded and three of the nine emission factors are unverified. A saving in accounted intensity need not equal avoided marginal emissions, and the ordering of arms may change under a marginal signal. The claim is limited to modeled operational emissions on average intensity. S3 refits the factor sets (2.04\,\% and 1.90\,\% for D on PM100), and X7 uses a fossil-only proxy.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{C. The oracle is not a global optimum.} The oracle is the same heuristic with perfect inputs, so the retained share is relative to it and has no slack-free meaning. We report the absolute saving and the slack curve before the share, and state the share per trace with its interval (Section~\ref{sec:method:inference}).

\medskip\noindent\textbf{D. No queue or capacity.} The replay has no queue, backfilling, idle power or interruptibility, and delayed jobs may concentrate load. The savings are bounds under free capacity, and interruptible jobs are not covered. X4 is a congestion indicator only: the hourly peak-to-mean ratio of D relative to run-now is \pendingkey{} on PM100, and it cannot establish feasibility.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{E. Interval scope.} The calendar interval is conditional on the observed job sample and treats BAs as independent; PM100 is one 2020 trace of 394 users after filtering. The synchronized-week interval (PH1) changes the width of the D interval by factors of 1.71, 0.93 and 2.28, while both intervals condition on the sampled jobs. The exploratory workload-sample check (S1) estimates the added workload-sample uncertainty and gives \pendingkey{} to \pendingkey{} for D on PM100.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{F. Carbon-weighted pooling.} The pooled estimand weights BAs by carbon, so high-carbon grids dominate, and every BA reuses the same job pool. The per-BA distribution of the runtime penalty (PH3) reports the spread beside the pooled value.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{G. Data vintage.} The planner assumes that the intensity of the hour ending at arrival is available at arrival, and the archived series holds revised values. EIA publishes generation by source about a day later and revises it afterwards \cite{eia930_about}. S2 delays the forecast information by 1 to 6 h and isolates delay, not revision, which remains unmeasured.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{H. Constant per-job power.} Power is a whole-job mean taken as constant, KIT FH2 carries 1 kW per job, and energy weighting favors long jobs (in the raw PM100 log, jobs over 24 h supply 14.1\,\% of the energy, but no analyzed PM100 job is that long). The unweighted outcome (the u rows of the ledger) and the job-length classes address it.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{I. Calendar alignment.} Each job lands in a random whole week of the test year, and the first and last days of the year (about 16 days in all) receive no arrivals. Three replicates and the S1 control show the spread.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{J. Weak runtime baselines.} The prediction (mean of the user's last two jobs) is a deliberately simple predictor, so H3 may understate the recovery of a better one, and the requested walltime is a loose bound by construction. X1 sweeps estimate accuracy, and the padded and quantile arms probe operator rules, both exploratory.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{K. Forecaster selection.} D was chosen on development MAE (it beat the seasonal naive day on 1 to 24 h error in 38 of 42 BAs), and MAE rank is not saving rank. Hyperparameters were frozen before the test year; S5 tunes the forecaster (exploratory), and PH6 compares D with the seven-day profile.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{L. Multiplicity.} Three confirmatory tests (H1 to H3, PM100, Holm) sit beside a large sensitivity grid. P values are given for H1 to H3 only, and the registry (Section~\ref{sec:method:registry}) and the all-cells table list every analysis.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{M. Selection and status limits.} The BA eligibility rule uses test-year availability, and KIT FH2 status codes cannot identify timeouts. S4 (secondary, run after the freeze; PREREG item 6, deviation row 2) includes timeouts on PM100 and gives 2.04\,\% for D, and a near-limit exclusion is reported.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{N. One heuristic.} The planner is a delay-only, non-interruptible policy with no spatial shifting. No scheduler is ranked, and the stated scope is the limit.

\subsection{Reproducibility}\label{sec:disc:repro}

A single script rebuilds every ledger from the stored result files and checks equality. The frozen scripts, the pre-registration and its checksum, the environment lock file, the checksum of every result file and the exact run commands are archived at [ZENODO\_DOI\_PLACEHOLDER], and the code is kept in a private repository ([GITHUB\_LINK\_PLACEHOLDER], to be supplied by the owner). Section~\ref{sec:method:registry} lists the post-freeze analyses with their status.


===== 07_conclusion =====
% 07_conclusion.tmpl.tex: SPEC s5 "7 Conclusion" (250 words). Written by A28. Results first, absolute before relative, no p-values, no new numbers.
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

This study replayed three real HPC job traces on 42 U.S. balancing authorities over a held-out year to measure how much carbon saving a delay planner keeps when it uses only a past-only forecast and the requested walltime. At 12 h of slack, this forecast-and-request policy gave a positive modeled saving on all three replays. It saved 6.6, 13.2 and 5.3 g CO$_2$e per kWh of job energy on PM100, F-DATA and KIT FH2, or 1.9\,\%, 3.8\,\% and 1.5\,\% of run-now emissions. The same planner with perfect information saved more, and the forecast-and-request policy kept 0.33, 0.53 and 0.38 of that saving. A history-based runtime prediction raised the saving on all three traces. The Shapley split of the remaining gap into a forecast term and a runtime term is descriptive: the runtime term was larger on PM100 and KIT FH2, and the terms were comparable on F-DATA.

The replay suggests that logging runtimes and adding a runtime predictor may be worth evaluating in production schedulers. The savings are bounds under free capacity, measured on production-average intensity rather than avoided marginal emissions, on foreign traces placed on U.S. grids, with intervals conditional on the sampled workload. No new scheduler is proposed.

Future work includes marginal-intensity signals, queue-aware simulation, a U.S. trace such as NLR Kestrel, and other forecast families. An exploratory hyperparameter search for the LightGBM forecaster changed the saving of the forecast-and-request policy by under 0.1 pp on each of the three traces, so tuning that model is unlikely to change the main result. Code, derived data and result ledgers are archived on Zenodo ([ZENODO DOI: owner to publish]); the source repository is private until publication ([GITHUB LINK: owner to provide]).


